The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```
static bool ltk_is_valid(struct mgmt_ltk_info *key)
{
	if (key->initiator != 0x00 && key->initiator != 0x01)
		return false;

	switch (key->addr.type) {
	case BDADDR_LE_PUBLIC:
		return true;

	case BDADDR_LE_RANDOM:
		/* Two most significant bits shall be set */
		if ((key->addr.bdaddr.b[5] & 0xc0) != 0xc0)
			return false;
		return true;
	}

	return false;
}
```
Reason: The `ltk_is_valid` function does not check that `key->enc_size` is within the size of the key buffer (`sizeof(key->val)`). An oversized `enc_size` passes validation and is stored. Later, when the stored LTK is used to reply to LE LTK requests, the `enc_size` is used to size stack operations, causing a buffer overflow because the key buffer is only 16 bytes.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```
+	if (key->enc_size > sizeof(key->val))
+		return false;
```
Reason: This additional validation ensures that any LTK with an `enc_size` larger than the key buffer size is rejected during load, preventing oversized values from being stored and later causing stack buffer overflows.

